From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:08:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hd textures: read and recolour replacements off the GL thread

Each new HD texture on screen was a hitch on the Vita's GL thread: 5-60 ms
reading its blob from the memory card, 10-15 ms waiting for the GPU between
mip levels, and 50 ms for a recoloured character (one per enemy that came
into view). The 1 % low frame rate of a mission was 6-9 fps.

- A loader thread per pack (HdConfig::async_load, <PREFIX>_HD_ASYNC=0 to
  turn it off) reads blobs through its own handle and builds recolours
  (decode, the palette the texture had when asked, the mip chain). The
  texture is drawn with its original meanwhile and the GL thread uploads
  the result at its next draw (GlHdTextures::ready). Results nobody claims
  are dropped past 8 MiB. The replayer keeps the old behaviour, so a replay
  still has every replacement from its first frame.
- The pack's index stays in RAM whole (32 bytes a texture): a lookup reads
  no file. The file is unbuffered: a blob is one seek and one read, where a
  buffered stream read it in BUFSIZ pieces.
- glp::reserve_compressed_mips (vitaGL patch 0004 in the Vita port): level 0
  of a compressed chain reserves the chain, so the levels go in place and
  the uploader no longer calls sceGxmTransferFinish between them.
- gl_timing logs a texture upload over 3 ms ([gl-time] slow texture upload:
  address, size, format, HD or not, and for a replacement the read and GPU
  wait), each texture at its 1st, 2nd, 4th... slow upload.

On the console: GPU waits of an HD upload 10-15 ms -> 0, a recolour on the
GL thread 50 ms -> 4-11 ms (the upload), 1 % low 6-9 -> 13-15 fps.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

