index 13b379f..83046a0 100644
--- a/docs/GL_RENDERER.md
+++ b/docs/GL_RENDERER.md
@@ -104,6 +104,14 @@ to 3.0 million instructions a frame and the walk from 6.0 to 3.5 million. Every
 What is left per GL draw is vitaGL's `glDrawElements` (about 15 µs on the console before this work, measured with
 `gl_timing`), which no change here touches.
 
+On the console the split renderer then showed what the instruction counts cannot: system calls. An event signal
+is a `sceKernelSetEventFlag` (3.9 µs, measured at start-up: the `costs:` line) and a clock read 0.7 µs, and the
+ring between the walker and the GL thread signalled twice a draw and read the clock twice a job. `GeWorker` now
+signals only a thread that may be asleep (a flag set before its last look at the ring), and a thread's busy time
+is the frame less its sleeps (`WalkIdle`, `GlIdle`), not the sum of timed jobs. Mission 1 went from 25 to about
+30 fps with no other change. With `gl_null_draw=1` (no draw calls, no GPU work) it runs at 36 fps with the GL
+thread 60 % busy: vitaGL's draw calls, with the state they flush, are about 13 ms of the GL thread's frame.
+
 ## Settings that matter on a Vita
 
 `display_scale` (2 = 960x544), `offscreen_scale` (1), `no_blend_color` (on), `depth_bits` (16), `writeback` (0),
@@ -210,6 +218,16 @@ every use:
 
 `texture_always_validate=1` is the old behaviour (hash and compare on every use) for comparison.
 
+**HD replacements are read off the GL thread** (`HdConfig::async_load`, `<PREFIX>_HD_ASYNC=0` turns it off; the
+replayer always turns it off, so a replay has every replacement from its first frame). The first time a texture
+with a replacement is drawn, its original is uploaded and the pack's loader thread is asked for the replacement:
+it reads the blob through its own handle of the pack and, for a recolour record, also decodes the image, applies
+the palette the texture had when it was asked for and builds the mip chain. At the texture's next draw after
+that (`GlHdTextures::ready`), the GL thread uploads the result. On the Vita a blob read from the memory card cost
+5-60 ms and a recolour 50 ms (one hitch per enemy that came on screen); the upload left on the GL thread is
+3-5 ms. The pack's whole index stays in RAM (32 bytes a texture) and the file is unbuffered, so a read is one
+seek and one read, and the index costs no file access at all.
+
 Numbers are in "Cost report" below.
 
 ## Recording and replaying frames
